{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Spiking Neuron.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Spiking Neuron.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"spiking neuron spiking neuron","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Spiking Neuron.md","fileName":"Spiking Neuron.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Spiking Neuron","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-15 02:20\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Emerging Computational Models]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Emerging Computational Models","Neuromorphic Computing","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Spiking Neuron.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Spiking Neuron.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"spiking neuron spiking neuron","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Spiking Neuron.md","fileName":"Spiking Neuron.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Spiking Neuron","heading":"Spiking Neuron","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A spiking neuron maintains an internal activation potential that changes as input pulses arrive and decays when they do not. It emits an output event only when the potential crosses a threshold. Timing therefore matters: enough inputs must occur within a suitable window to trigger a spike.\n\nKelleher contrasts this event-driven unit with a standard artificial neuron that computes an activation from the inputs at each processing step. In [[Neuromorphic Computing]], sparse firing can reduce repeated activity and energy use. It is a hardware-oriented computational model inspired by biological signaling, not a claim that all neural-network tasks are better expressed with spikes.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Emerging Computational Models","Neuromorphic Computing","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Spiking Neuron.md::2","sourceK